{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791128327,"nonce":"ducIkupbi0hkYRTJFKnB0P8ytUU=","title":"word-spacing is measured but never drawn","message":"Both measurers add (word_count - 1) * word_spacing to a run's width:\nEstimatedTextMeasurer in blinc_layout/src/text_measure.rs, and\nFontTextMeasurer::estimate_size in text_measurer.rs. blinc_text applies\nit nowhere: there is no reference to word_spacing anywhere under\nblinc_text/src, and layout.rs advances by x_advance + letter_spacing\nonly.\n\nSo a word-spaced run measures wider than it draws, and the spacing never\nappears on screen. The box is too wide by (words - 1) * word_spacing,\nwhich pushes centred text off centre in the other direction from the\nletter-spacing case.\n\nThe API exposes it: Text::word_spacing sets it, RenderProps and\nTextElement carry it, and the CSS parser accepts word-spacing.\n\nTwo ways to make it consistent, and this needs a decision rather than a\npatch:\n\n1. Apply it in blinc_text's layout, after each space glyph, which is\n   what CSS means. Text that already sets word-spacing would start\n   rendering wider, changing existing layouts.\n2. Drop it from the measurers and treat it as unimplemented, which makes\n   boxes correct immediately and leaves the modifier doing nothing until\n   someone implements it.\n\nFound while fixing the letter-spacing count (the measure counted the\ngaps between characters while the renderer advanced after every\ncharacter). That one was safe to fix because the renderer's behaviour\nwas already the CSS one and only the estimator disagreed. This one has\nno correct side to converge on.","files":null}]}